{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-09-18T09:00:47.933Z","tool_name":"invoke_agent","tool_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8fd171fc7c3da9aa","parameters":{"agent_name":"codebase_investigator","prompt":"CODEBASE TRACE for Jira ticket GMT-21241 — Three cronjobs in ABORTED status on Samsung Toko Hybris platform (SMI/MENA region).\n\nThe three cronjobs are:\n1. toko-iq-ecom-flat-c360-reports\n2. full-backofficeIndex-CronJob\n3. kwMktCPIProductCategoryOutboundSyncCronJob\n\nFor EACH cronjob, trace the following in the codebase at C:\\toko\\toko6\\toko6\\core-customize\\hybris\\bin:\n\n1. Find the cronjob's Java class — search for the cronjob name in spring XML configs, *-spring.xml files, and impex files to find the bean definition and associated class.\n2. Find the JobPerformable class and its perform() method.\n3. Identify what the cronjob does — what data it syncs, what indexes it builds, what reports it generates.\n4. Look for common failure modes — database timeouts, OutOfMemory risks, large batch operations, external API calls that could timeout.\n5. Search for any known issues or TODO comments near the cronjob code.\n\nUse grep_search to find:\n- The cronjob bean definitions: search for \"toko-iq-ecom-flat-c360-reports\", \"full-backofficeIndex-CronJob\", \"kwMktCPIProductCategoryOutboundSyncCronJob\" in *.xml and *.impex files\n- The Java classes: search for class names derived from bean definitions\n- Any abort/error handling code\n\nAlso search for the generic cronjob abort mechanism in Hybris — what causes a cronjob to go to ABORTED status (vs ERROR or RUNNING). Look for AbortHandledJob or similar.\n\nReturn for each cronjob:\n- Bean definition file and line\n- JobPerformable class name and file path\n- What the cronjob does (brief)\n- Known failure modes\n- Any relevant code snippets","wait_for_previous":false}}
